Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — source-developed working scene
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-10-10
Status: Independent screenplay development under DEC-0019; refinements during production

INT. WESTMERE COMPLIANCE OFFICE - MONDAY MORNING

08:07. Elaine Foster receives North Vale's preservation request. Do not destroy, amend or migrate specified historic records. She opens H09-55216.

Migrated summary: protected transfer, permanent placement, closed.

Older material: maternal contact, reunification work, another review due. Two days later an external authority changed the case.

Elaine calls Martin Hale, retired compliance administrator.

MARTIN (PHONE)
We sent things to an extension. Somebody called back. Status changed.

ELAINE
Did you contact the issuing authority?

MARTIN (PHONE)
That wasn't the process.

His notebook contains changing extensions and procedures. No child records.

ELAINE
Keep it. Don't give it to anyone yet.

She checks a small sample. A lawful return home. A lawful permanent placement. Then another case routed through the suspect legal service.

Her incident note distinguishes legitimate restricted confidentiality from a subset whose independent authority cannot yet be verified.

INT. WESTMERE ARCHIVE DESK - LATE MORNING

Seven boxes wait on a trolley. A polite courier presents an urgent legal reconciliation order issued today, after the hold.

COURIER
I'm collecting what I've been told to collect.

ELAINE
I understand. These aren't being released.

He asks about scans. Photographs of labels. She refuses both. Checks the authorising office against Martin's old service range.

She signs: RELEASE REFUSED — PRESERVATION HOLD ACTIVE. 11:48.

COURIER
Am I in trouble?

ELAINE
As far as I know, no.

He leaves with his refusal copy. Elaine attaches the collection order to the incident.

INT. CONTRACTOR WORKROOM - DAY

Priya asks for a supported account of how a contractor verified an order.

VOSS
Written. One case. Properly redacted.

VALE
They're asking the right question.

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE - DAY

Helen calls Mac. Brian watches from the kitchen doorway.

HELEN
You kept telling us it was handled. Did you tell them where she was?

MAC (PHONE)
I told them the placement was stable.

HELEN
She has a name. Use it.

A pause.

MAC (PHONE)
Zoe.

HELEN
If you call again, I'm not answering.

She ends the call. No triumphant relief.

INT. WESTMERE COMPLIANCE OFFICE - AFTERNOON

Elaine speaks directly with North Vale and both organisations' current legal advisers. No Meridian intermediary.

NORTH VALE LAWYER (SPEAKER)
The base reference is real. We cannot support the supplemental powers. Could Westmere delegate recovery powers to a private sponsor?

ELAINE
No.

NORTH VALE LAWYER (SPEAKER)
Then what authorised the move?

ELAINE
I don't know.

EXT. RESERVOIR CAR PARK - AFTERNOON

Zoe reads the logged box refusal. A dog ignores its owner's thrown ball.

ZOE
They wrote it down themselves.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
We can't control what they ask next.

ELIAS
No.

The backpack is at her feet. For the first time, it is one record among several.

ZOE
What do we control?

ELIAS
What we disclose. Whether we stay here.

She checks the time and agrees to move on.
